**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 25-40 documents across Thai PBS, Freedom House, HRW, Amnesty, OHCHR, GOV.UK, Guardian/Reuters-family coverage, AFP fact-checking, Bangkok Post, The Active, SCMP, AP, and Reuters Connect.
- I kept the most useful non-duplicative evidence on protest policing, emergency powers, prosecution of dissent, torture/disappearance reform, and crisis response.
- I rejected duplicate APEC protest coverage when it repeated the same event without adding new factual content, especially photo-only or republished Reuters assets.
- I rejected out-of-period material from 2020-2021 when it did not materially improve the 2022 record.
- I rejected purely international or diplomatic Thailand material that did not connect to domestic safety, and I avoided re-fetching the structured rights datasets already present in your local prior.
- I also discarded a few cache-miss pages from Thai PBS/The Active and one Amnesty page that did not open cleanly, because the same factual ground was covered by accessible official or NGO sources.
- The retained set gives both negative and favorable signals, but the negatives are broader and more repeated than the positives.

**Remaining gaps**
- I still do not have direct cabinet minutes or an internal order showing Prayut personally directed specific police actions, surveillance choices, or prosecutions.
- The evidence is strong on protest repression and emergency-law abuse, but weaker on day-to-day prison conditions, complaint handling, and whether the new torture law changed behavior before year-end 2022.
- The southern-border conflict material shows continuing security-force abuse and insurgent violence, but not a clean ruler-level command trail for every incident.
- More search would help if you want tighter attribution for particular police operations, specific detention cases, or implementation of the late-2022 reform laws.
